eady exposed: previous player has [W1, W2, W3], next has [W2, W3, W4], and across has discarded several Ws. Only limited Ws remain (e.g., W5:1, W6:4, etc.), but W1-W4 are heavily depleted.\n\nMoreover, you have a **Pung of T8**, and the T-suit is nearly exhausted\u2014many T-tiles are discarded or melded. This reduces chance of further T-based development.\n\nBest course: discard a non-essential, low-risk tile to avoid feeding opponents\u2019 chows while keeping flexibility.\n\nReason:  \nAmong the options, **W8** stands out as the safest discard. It's isolated in your hand, not forming any sequence with other W-tiles (gap between W4 and W8), and not a terminal (9) or honor. Opponents have not shown interest in high-numbered W-tiles. Also, W8 is less likely to complete a chow like [W7, W8, W9] since W7 is still available (4 unshown), but no one has shown W7 or W9 melds. Discarding W8 minimizes risk compared to W1-W4, which are central to multiple active chows.\n\nAdditionally, holding onto W1, W2, W3, W4 gives potential for building Character-based chows toward **Full Flush** (24 points), especially if you draw W5-W7. Keeping T5 and B3 allows for future flexibility, though they are less ideal.\n\nAnswer: Play W8"}
